  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Periods of rain continue into tonight associated\nwith the remnants of Isaias \n\nSteady light to moderate rain (rates generally <0.50 in/hr) will\nbe most persistent along and east of the Allegheny Front this\nevening into tonight. Increasing MUCAPE and elevated instability\ncould result in some locally heavy, short duration rain/rates \nacross south central PA into tonight. Much of this area has \nalready picked up 1-2\" since Saturday evening, so we still can't\nrule out some isolated runoff problems. Otherwise, the prolonged\ndry run leading up to this Isaias rain event has allowed a \nsufficient cushion for what has largely been a duration type \nheavy rain episode with some locations ticking over 3 inches in\nthe past 24 hours. Current MA/OH RFC river guidance keeps all \nfcst points below action stages, thanks to very low flows at the\nstart of the event.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Big temperature swing forecast for the week ahead \n\nNoticeably warmer and more humid conditions expected early this\nweek with max temps on Tuesday forecast to peak 10-15 degrees \nabove the historical average for mid October and possibly\nchallenge daily records with highs in the 70-80F range. \n\nImproving weather conditions can be expected Monday as the \nremnants of Isaias exit the Mid Atlantic. Rain chances on \nTuesday appear to be maximized over the northern tier where a \nshortwave aloft and sfc cold front (infused with moisture from \nTS Rachel?) streak eastward from the Great Lakes. SPC has\nhighlighted this area with a conditional low risk level 1/5 for\nsevere t-storms. Another period of rain is likely late in the \nweek, but exact timing remains uncertain. \n\nConfidence is increasing in a seasonably chilly cooldown and big\ntemperature swing (vs. early in the week) for the upcoming\nweekend. The substantial cooldown appears to be delivered in \nthe wake of a strong cold front by gusty west-northwest flow \ndirecting the colder air over the Great Lakes. This could setup\nsome lake effect/upslope rain or snow showers (yes, first flakes\npossible in the Alleghenies/Laurel Highlands) followed by frost\nand freeze conditions. To quantify the magnitude of the fcst \ntemp swing this week, Bradford could go from 75 degrees Tuesday \nafternoon to 25 degrees by early Sunday morning (50 degree \nswing!)",
